  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + z²) / 2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss with contour lines. It is steeper along x, so overshoot appears there first: zigzags above learning rate 0.4, and growing swings (divergence) above 0.8.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per direction has spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Orange arrow: the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey arrow: −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at 1.5 × their true length in bowl units. 'Mini-batch step length' is the true length.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. 'New run, keep last path' carries on with the next noise values, so its noise is different.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished). It ends early as diverged when the loss passes 5 (the loss at the bowl's steep edge) or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. The loss readout always shows the true value.",
    "The ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed. If you change a setting during a run, it applies from the next step on."
  ],
